{"subjectId":"c","id":"pointer-to-pointer","title":"Pointers to Pointers","group":"Pointers","order":25,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>T **</code> points to a <code>T *</code>. You need one whenever a function must change <em>which</em> object a caller’s pointer refers to — and for arrays of strings like <code>argv</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 5;\n    int *p = &amp;x;\n    int **pp = &amp;p;\n    **pp = 50;                         /* x = 50 */\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", x, *p, **pp);\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", *pp == p, p == &amp;x);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>50 50 50\n1 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>pp</code> (<code>int **</code>)</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>p</code> (<code>int *</code>)</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>x</code> (<code>int</code>) = 50</div></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back-up\"></i>Changing the caller's pointer</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\n/* allocate and hand back through an out-parameter */\nint make_array(int **out, int n) {\n    int *a = malloc(n * sizeof *a);\n    if (!a) return -1;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) a[i] = i * 10;\n    *out = a;                        /* modifies the caller's pointer */\n    return 0;\n}\n\nvoid free_and_null(int **p) {\n    free(*p);\n    *p = NULL;                       /* caller's pointer no longer dangles */\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int *data = NULL;\n    if (make_array(&amp;data, 4) == 0) printf(\"%d %d\\n\", data[1], data[3]);\n    free_and_null(&amp;data);\n    printf(\"data is %s\\n\", data == NULL ? \"NULL\" : \"set\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 30\ndata is NULL</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>Dynamic 2-D arrays (jagged)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int rows = 3;\n    int **tri = malloc(rows * sizeof *tri);          /* array of row pointers */\n    if (!tri) return 1;\n    for (int r = 0; r &lt; rows; r++) {\n        tri[r] = malloc((r + 1) * sizeof **tri);      /* each row its own length */\n        if (!tri[r]) return 1;\n        for (int c = 0; c &lt;= r; c++)\n            tri[r][c] = (c == 0 || c == r) ? 1 : tri[r - 1][c - 1] + tri[r - 1][c];\n    }\n    for (int r = 0; r &lt; rows; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c &lt;= r; c++) printf(\"%d \", tri[r][c]);\n        printf(\"\\n\");\n        free(tri[r]);                                  /* free rows first... */\n    }\n    free(tri);                                         /* ...then the spine */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1\n1 1\n1 2 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A contiguous alternative is one <code>malloc(rows * cols * sizeof(int))</code> indexed as <code>a[r * cols + c]</code>, or a pointer to a VLA row type: <code>int (*m)[cols] = malloc(rows * sizeof *m);</code> — one allocation, one <code>free</code>, cache-friendly.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Linked-list insertion with <code>**</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nstruct node { int val; struct node *next; };\n\n/* inserting at the head changes the caller's head pointer */\nvoid push(struct node **head, int v) {\n    struct node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);\n    if (!n) return;\n    n-&gt;val = v;\n    n-&gt;next = *head;\n    *head = n;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    struct node *head = NULL;\n    push(&amp;head, 3); push(&amp;head, 2); push(&amp;head, 1);\n    for (struct node *n = head; n; n = n-&gt;next) printf(\"%d \", n-&gt;val);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    while (head) { struct node *next = head-&gt;next; free(head); head = next; }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 3</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When do you need a double pointer?</summary><div><p>When a function must modify a pointer owned by the caller (allocate into it, reset it to <code>NULL</code>, change a list head), and to represent arrays of pointers — <code>char **argv</code>, jagged 2-D arrays, arrays of strings.</p></div></details></section>"}